{"status":"success","execution":{"requested":["concurrency","backups"],"reservedAttempts":2,"confirmedLaunches":2,"indeterminate":0,"exactCountMet":true,"completed":["concurrency","backups"],"incomplete":[],"outcomes":[{"assignment":"concurrency","path":"/home/balauru/.pi-profiles/fabric/tmp/fabric-research-rework-review/behavior-evaluator-final/live-run-20260328-a-cde/worker-concurrency.json","id":"40ab07cb56ee491d8de2b707f273246f","status":"completed","error":null,"usage":{"input":23315,"output":4566,"cacheRead":49152,"cacheWrite":0,"cost":0.1112524},"hasValue":true},{"assignment":"backups","path":"/home/balauru/.pi-profiles/fabric/tmp/fabric-research-rework-review/behavior-evaluator-final/live-run-20260328-a-cde/worker-backups.json","id":"e664e4475cc64057b8fba1e075912e88","status":"completed","error":null,"usage":{"input":29174,"output":5096,"cacheRead":51200,"cacheWrite":0,"cost":0.12974},"hasValue":true}]},"coverage":[{"slot":"concurrency","status":"supported","rowIds":["main/sqlite-reader-writer-overlap","main/postgres-reader-writer-overlap"],"reason":"Both current official documents were independently fetched by Main."},{"slot":"deployment","status":"supported","rowIds":["main/sqlite-wal-network-boundary","main/postgres-network-client-server"],"reason":"Both topology facts were independently fetched by Main."},{"slot":"backup","status":"supported","rowIds":["main/sqlite-online-backup","main/postgres-online-base-backup"],"reason":"Both online-backup mechanism statements were independently fetched by Main."},{"slot":"operational-risk","status":"supported","rowIds":["main/postgres-wal-method-conditions"],"reason":"Main independently inspected current pg_basebackup WAL retention/stream conditions; SQLite active-WAL backup-copy details remain a stated limitation."}],"evidence":[{"id":"main/sqlite-reader-writer-overlap","slot":"concurrency","finding":"SQLite WAL documentation says readers do not block writers and a writer does not block readers; reading and writing can proceed concurrently.","status":"documented_fact","support":[{"url":"https://www.sqlite.org/wal.html","passage":"WAL provides more concurrency as readers do not block writers and a writer does not block readers. Reading and writing can proceed concurrently.","sourceType":"Primary documentation"}],"method":"Applies to WAL mode; not a throughput measurement.","comparability":"Comparable only to reader/writer overlap.","confidence":"High: Main inspected the current official page.","disposition":"retain","citations":["https://www.sqlite.org/wal.html"]},{"id":"main/postgres-reader-writer-overlap","slot":"concurrency","finding":"PostgreSQL MVCC documentation says reading never blocks writing and writing never blocks reading.","status":"documented_fact","support":[{"url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/mvcc-intro.html","passage":"In MVCC locks acquired for querying (reading) data do not conflict with locks acquired for writing data, and so reading never blocks writing and writing never blocks reading.","sourceType":"Primary documentation"}],"method":"Documented MVCC behavior; no writer-contention performance conclusion.","comparability":"Comparable only to reader/writer overlap.","confidence":"High: Main inspected the current official page.","disposition":"retain","citations":["https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/mvcc-intro.html"]},{"id":"main/sqlite-wal-network-boundary","slot":"deployment","finding":"SQLite WAL requires all database-using processes on the same host and does not work over a network filesystem.","status":"documented_fact","support":[{"url":"https://www.sqlite.org/wal.html","passage":"All processes using a database must be on the same host computer; WAL does not work over a network filesystem.","sourceType":"Primary documentation"}],"method":"Applies to direct access in WAL mode.","comparability":"A file/shared-memory constraint, not a performance result.","confidence":"High: Main inspected the current official page.","disposition":"retain","citations":["https://www.sqlite.org/wal.html"]},{"id":"main/postgres-network-client-server","slot":"deployment","finding":"PostgreSQL documents client/server deployment across hosts over TCP/IP and multiple concurrent client connections.","status":"documented_fact","support":[{"url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial-arch.html","passage":"the client and the server can be on different hosts. In that case they communicate over a TCP/IP network connection. The PostgreSQL server can handle multiple concurrent connections from clients.","sourceType":"Primary documentation"}],"method":"Documented architecture, not capacity sizing.","comparability":"Shows a different access topology than SQLite WAL.","confidence":"High: Main inspected the current official page.","disposition":"retain","citations":["https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial-arch.html"]},{"id":"main/sqlite-online-backup","slot":"backup","finding":"SQLite's Online Backup API can back up a live source without preventing other connections from reading or writing the source during the backup.","status":"documented_fact","support":[{"url":"https://www.sqlite.org/c3ref/backup_finish.html","passage":"The source database is read-locked only while it is being read; it is not locked continuously for the entire backup operation. Thus, the backup may be performed on a live source database without preventing other database connections from reading or writing to the source database while the backup is underway.","sourceType":"Primary documentation"}],"method":"API-level locking semantics; not a restore test.","comparability":"Not a matched performance comparison with pg_basebackup.","confidence":"High: Main inspected the current official page.","disposition":"retain","citations":["https://www.sqlite.org/c3ref/backup_finish.html"]},{"id":"main/postgres-online-base-backup","slot":"backup","finding":"PostgreSQL documents pg_basebackup as a base backup of a running cluster that does not affect other database clients and can support PITR or a standby.","status":"documented_fact","support":[{"url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/app-pgbasebackup.html","passage":"pg_basebackup is used to take a base backup of a running PostgreSQL database cluster. The backup is taken without affecting other clients of the database, and can be used both for point-in-time recovery and as the starting point for a log-shipping or streaming-replication standby server.","sourceType":"Primary documentation"}],"method":"Physical-cluster backup documentation; deployment settings remain untested.","comparability":"Not a matched performance comparison with SQLite's API.","confidence":"High for documented availability; qualified for deployment correctness.","disposition":"qualify","citations":["https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/app-pgbasebackup.html"]},{"id":"main/postgres-wal-method-conditions","slot":"operational-risk","finding":"For pg_basebackup WAL method fetch, needed WAL must remain available until collection completes or the backup fails and is unusable; stream uses a second replication connection and requires the client to keep up with WAL.","status":"documented_fact","support":[{"url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/app-pgbasebackup.html","passage":"If the required log data has been recycled before it's time to transfer it, the backup will fail and be unusable. Stream write-ahead log data while the backup is being taken. This method will open a second connection to the server and start streaming the write-ahead log in parallel while running the backup. Therefore, it will require two replication connections not just one. As long as the client can keep up with the write-ahead log data, using this method requires no extra write-ahead logs to be saved on the source server.","sourceType":"Primary documentation"}],"method":"Applies to the named pg_basebackup WAL methods, not measured reliability.","comparability":"No equivalence to SQLite WAL files or restore testin